---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTASection from '@/components/CTASection.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '@/lib/site';

const options = [
  {
    title: 'Export cartons',
    description:
      'Rigid export-grade cartons calibrated to standard pallet configurations, protecting fruit through long-distance transit.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Interior packaging',
    description:
      'Protective liners and trays that reduce abrasion and keep fruit firmly positioned through handling and transport.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Retail-ready packs',
    description:
      'Optional pre-pack configurations for buyers serving supermarket and retail channels.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Labeling & branding',
    description:
      'Carton marking and labeling prepared to destination-market requirements, including buyer branding where agreed.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Iranian Apple Packaging for Export — Cartons & Packing | SibExport"
  description="Export apple packaging from SibExport: carton types, ventilation, palletization, labeling and buyer-specific packing for Iranian apple exports."
  path="/apple-packaging/"
  ogImage="/images/packaging.png"
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Apple Packaging' }]}
  />
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Packaging"
    title="Iranian apple packaging for export"
    description="Good fruit deserves good packaging. Every SibExport shipment is packed to survive long-distance transit and arrive in premium condition."
  />

  <article class="container-site py-14 lg:py-20">
    <div class="prose-site mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h2>Packaging is product protection, not presentation</h2>
      <p>
        In domestic trade, an apple only has to survive the trip from orchard
        to market stall. In export, it has to survive being picked, graded,
        packed, palletized, driven across borders, transshipped, cleared
        through customs and distributed — and still look like premium fruit
        when a customer picks it up. Packaging is what stands between those
        two outcomes.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why SibExport treats packing specification as seriously as
        fruit specification. The two are agreed together, in the same written
        document, before an order begins.
      </p>

      <h2>Carton types and construction</h2>

<figure class="my-8">
      <img
        src="/images/banner-packing.svg"
        alt="Export cartons and packaging for Iranian apples"
        width="1200"
        height="480"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full rounded-xl border border-border shadow-sm"
      />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-muted-foreground">Export cartons and packaging for Iranian apples</figcaption>
    </figure>
      <p>
        Export apples ship in rigid, corrugated cartons engineered for stacked
        weight and long transit. Carton specification is not generic: wall
        strength, dimensions and vent placement are selected for the fruit
        category, the packing pattern and the journey's length and climate.
        A carton bound for a short regional road journey faces different
        demands than one crossing multiple borders or moving by sea.
      </p>
      <p>
        Carton dimensions are coordinated with pallet footprint so that
        stacked loads remain stable and air can circulate between cartons.
        The exact carton specification for your order — dimensions, strength,
        vent pattern — is confirmed in writing before production.
      </p>

      <h2>Ventilation and cooling</h2>
      <p>
        Apples continue to breathe after harvest, producing heat and
        moisture. Packaging has to manage that biology, not fight it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Vent patterns</strong> allow air to move through stacked
          cartons, preventing heat build-up in the load
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pre-cooling</strong> before packing removes field heat so
          fruit enters transit at the right temperature
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cold-chain continuity</strong> — refrigerated transport
          where the route requires it — keeps the fruit's condition stable
          from loading to arrival
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The packaging and cooling plan is matched to the destination's
        climate and transit length as part of order preparation.
      </p>

      <h2>Protection during transport</h2>
      <p>
        Inside the carton, interior packaging does the quiet work: liners and
        trays that immobilize fruit, prevent bruising from vibration, and
        separate delicate categories — golden apples in particular — from
        handling damage. Interior configuration is selected per category:
        what protects a firm green apple is not what a thin-skinned golden
        apple needs.
      </p>

      <h2>Labeling and marking</h2>
      <p>
        Export cartons carry the marking your destination market and customs
        clearance require: product identification, count or net weight,
        origin, traceability marking and handling pictograms. Where buyers
        have their own brand program, buyer branding on cartons can be
        discussed and agreed as part of the order. Labeling content is
        confirmed during order preparation so cartons arrive compliant, not
        corrected.
      </p>

      <h2>Palletization</h2>
      <p>
        For many destinations, cartons are palletized for stability and
        faster handling: a fixed carton-per-layer pattern, stretch or net
        stabilization, and pallet counts matched to container or truck
        capacity. Pallet configuration affects both load stability and
        temperature behavior inside the load, so it is planned — not
        improvised at the loading dock. Your destination's handling
        infrastructure determines the right approach, and it is confirmed in
        the packing plan.
      </p>

      <h2>Buyer-specific packaging requirements</h2>
      <p>
        Established markets often have their own expectations: a preferred
        carton count, a net weight convention, a retail-ready format, a
        language requirement for labels. All of these are ordinary parts of
        an order. When you request a quotation, include any packaging
        requirement your market expects — it goes straight into the
        specification sheet, and the packing plan is built around it.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <strong>Subject to confirmation:</strong> packaging configurations are
        agreed per order. Carton formats and pack types available for a given
        shipment depend on the season, the category and the destination — and
        are always confirmed in writing before packing begins.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>How packaging fits the wider order</h2>
      <p>
        Packaging sits in the middle of the export workflow: fruit is
        specified, sourced and graded first; the packing plan is then
        executed and verified at loading. The full sequence — from your
        inquiry to delivered cargo — is laid out step by step on the
        <a href="/apple-export-process/">export process</a> page, and the
        grading standards fruit must meet before it is packed are covered
        under <a href="/apple-quality-specifications/">quality and
        specifications</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your market's packing standard is specific and non-negotiable,
        that is exactly the kind of detail we want early. Send it with your
        quotation request — via the form or WhatsApp
        {CONTACT.whatsappInternational} — and it will be part of the offer you
        receive.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection
    title="Discuss your packaging requirements"
    description="Share your destination and channel requirements to receive packing options and a tailored quotation."
  />
</BaseLayout>
